KUBERNETES CONFIGURATION: IMPERATIVE vs DECLARATIVE

Imperative  =(kubectl create / set / run)  -> fast
Declarative =(YAML + kubectl apply)       -> repeatable

Note: generate YAML imperatively, then edit and apply it

  kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=ENV=prod --dry-run=client -o yaml > cm.yaml
  kubectl apply -f cm.yaml

1) CONFIGMAP (non-sensitive) 
IMPERATIVE
  kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=ENV=prod --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug
  kubectl create configmap app-env --from-env-file=.env
  kubectl create configmap app-file --from-file=app.properties
  kubectl get cm app-config -o yaml

DECLARATIVE
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: ConfigMap
  metadata:
    name: app-config
  data:
    ENV: prod
    LOG_LEVEL: debug

Use in a Pod (3 ways)
  a) one key as env var   -> env.valueFrom.configMapKeyRef
  b) all keys as env vars -> envFrom.configMapRef
  c) as files             -> volumes.configMap + volumeMounts

  Imperative way to inject into a Deployment:
  kubectl set env deployment/nginx --from=configmap/app-env

2) SECRET (sensitive data)  
IMPERATIVE (kubectl does the base64 for you)
  kubectl create secret generic db-secret --from-literal=DB_USER=admin --from-literal=DB_PASS='S3cr3t'
  kubectl get secret db-secret -o jsonpath='{.data.DB_PASS}' | base64 -d 

DECLARATIVE
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Secret
  metadata:
    name: db-secret
  type: Opaque
  stringData:          
    DB_USER: admin
    DB_PASS: S3cr3t  

3) VOLUMES  

Declarative only:
  volumes:
  - name: cache
    emptyDir: {}
  containers:
  - name: web
    volumeMounts:
    - name: cache
      mountPath: /cache 

4) PROBES  
  startupProbe    did the app start?     fail = restart, other probes wait
  readinessProbe  can it take traffic?   fail = removed from Service endpoints, NO restart
  livenessProbe   is it alive?           fail = container restart

Probes have no imperative command. Write YAML (or kubectl edit):
  livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /
    port: 80
  initialDelaySeconds: 10
  periodSeconds: 10

  readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /
    port: 80
  periodSeconds: 5

  startupProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /
    port: 80
  initialDelaySeconds: 0
  periodSeconds: 5
  failureThreshold: 30

initialDelaySeconds: 0 – Start checking immediately.
periodSeconds: 5 – Check every 5 seconds.
failureThreshold: 30 – Allow 30 consecutive failures before restarting the container.

5) REQUESTS vs LIMITS

IMPERATIVE
  kubectl set resources deployment/nginx --requests=cpu=100m,memory=64Mi --limits=cpu=200m,memory=128Mi

DECLARATIVE
  resources:
    requests: {cpu: 100m, memory: 64Mi}
    limits:   {cpu: 200m, memory: 128Mi}
